  <div class="prose">
    <h3>What you can do</h3>
    <p>Use, study, change and share Ferrite for any noncommercial purpose. The licence names hobby projects and
      amateur pursuits, and noncommercial organisations such as charities, schools, public safety and government.
      Hams, clubs and emergency groups are covered.</p>

    <h3>What needs permission</h3>
    <p>Selling Ferrite, shipping it on radios for sale, or selling a service built on it needs a separate commercial
      licence from the copyright holder. Radio manufacturers: see <a href="/bench/#manufacturers">working with
      us</a>.</p>

    <h3>Why this licence</h3>
    <p>AI tools now make forking and repackaging firmware nearly effortless. We want the work, the fixes and the
      people to stay in one place, where everyone benefits, rather than split across many copies. And nobody should
      be able to take Ferrite and sell it.</p>
    <p>You can still change Ferrite and share your changes for noncommercial use. We'd rather those changes come
      back to the project, as bug reports, ideas or contributions on the <a href="{{ site.discord }}">Discord</a>,
      so everyone's radio gets them.</p>
    <p>Because it rules out commercial use, it isn't "open source" in the OSI's sense. We say free, with its source
      public.</p>

    <h3>Contributions</h3>
    <p>A contributor agreement is in draft.</p>

    <p class="note">This page is a summary. The <a href="{{ site.licence.url }}">licence text</a> is what counts.</p>
